%%%% EVAL
We report mean compliance, graded binary for four tasks as in the original paper, and strict passes. Our main CoT-Control number is the mean over the seven tasks that are not character-level (all but alternating case, lowercase and uppercase; 350 pairs): across their eight models, \citet{haskins2026obfuscation} find that only these seven predict obfuscation (composite $r=0.898$, against $0.800$ for all ten; $r=0.011$, $-0.197$ and $-0.469$ for the three case tasks). We adopted this composite after the first version of this paper, which used the ten-task mean; every table also reports the ten-task numbers.
%%%% QWEN1
On the seven non-character CoT-Control tasks, mean compliance rises from [[mean7_Base]] to [[mean7_SFT_seed_42]], [[mean7_SFT_seed_1]] and [[mean7_SFT_seed_2]] (gains of [[gain7_SFT_seed_42|ci]]; [[gain7_SFT_seed_1|ci]]; [[gain7_SFT_seed_2|ci]]), and strict passes from [[strict7_Base]] to [[strict7_SFT_seed_42]], [[strict7_SFT_seed_1]] and [[strict7_SFT_seed_2]] of 350; over all ten tasks the mean rises from [[mean10_Base]] to [[mean10_SFT_seed_42]], [[mean10_SFT_seed_1]] and [[mean10_SFT_seed_2]].
%%%% QWEN2
Within the seven tasks, the seed-42 gain is on third person ([[task_mean_Base_third_person]]\% to [[task_mean_SFT_seed_42_third_person]]\%), word suppression ([[task_mean_Base_word_suppression]]\% to [[task_mean_SFT_seed_42_word_suppression]]\%), multiple-word suppression ([[task_mean_Base_multiple_word_suppression]]\% to [[task_mean_SFT_seed_42_multiple_word_suppression]]\%) and repeat sentences ([[task_mean_Base_repeat_sentences]]\% to [[task_mean_SFT_seed_42_repeat_sentences]]\%); arrow prefix, end of sentence and meow stay at or near zero as graded. Much of the larger ten-task gain comes from uppercase, a case task (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:standalone-task}).
%%%% PHI
\paragraph{Phi-4-reasoning.} SFT raises ReasonIF IFS from 5.0\% to 12.0\%, almost entirely on the word-limit constraint (without it, $+1.6$, $[-0.4, 3.6]$), and seven-task CoT-Control compliance from [[mean7_Phi-4-reasoning_Base]] to [[mean7_Phi-4-reasoning_SFT]] ([[gain7_Phi-4-reasoning_SFT|ci]]; ten tasks: [[mean10_Phi-4-reasoning_Base]] to [[mean10_Phi-4-reasoning_SFT]]), but on the first 100 words no gain remains ([[gain7_first100_Phi-4-reasoning_SFT|ci]]; Section~\ref{sec:length}), so the gain is explained by shorter outputs.
%%%% OLMO
\paragraph{OLMo-3-7B-Think.} With the Qwen recipe, ReasonIF IFS rises from 8.3\% to 11.7\%, but the seven-task CoT-Control mean does not change ([[gain7_OLMo-3-7B-Think_SFT_lr1e-4|ci]]; [[gain7_degen0_OLMo-3-7B-Think_SFT_lr1e-4|est]] with degenerate traces scored 0); its ten-task gain ([[gain10_OLMo-3-7B-Think_SFT_lr1e-4|est]]) rested mostly on degenerate passes (14 of its 19 strict passes). With learning rate $2\times10^{-4}$ the style transfers (407 of 500 traces open with a bold heading) and controllability moves in both directions: lowercase rises from 6.2 to 35.5 and uppercase from 4.3 to 16.5, while third person falls from 87.2 to 52.7, plausibly because the training reasoning is written in the first person, so the seven-task mean falls ([[gain7_OLMo-3-7B-Think_SFT_lr2e-4|ci]]; ten tasks [[gain10_OLMo-3-7B-Think_SFT_lr2e-4|ci]]; Appendix Tables~\ref{tab:olmo} and~\ref{tab:olmo-task}). On ReasonIF,
%%%% LENGTH
On the seven tasks the Qwen gains are [[gain7_first100_SFT_seed_42|est]], [[gain7_first100_SFT_seed_1|est]] and [[gain7_first100_SFT_seed_2|est]] points for the three seeds, against [[gain7_SFT_seed_42|est]], [[gain7_SFT_seed_1|est]] and [[gain7_SFT_seed_2|est]] over the full traces (ten tasks: $+8.60$, $+9.35$ and $+9.39$ on the first 100 words). This keeps [[gain7_first100_SFT_seed_42|n]] of 350 pairs for seed 42; requiring 200 or 300 words keeps [[gain7_first200_SFT_seed_42|n]] and [[gain7_first300_SFT_seed_42|n]] pairs and gives [[gain7_first200_SFT_seed_42|ci]] and [[gain7_first300_SFT_seed_42|ci]].
%%%% LENGTH2
Length therefore explains part of the Qwen gain, but a gain remains for every seed. For Phi the same check removes the gain ([[gain7_first100_Phi-4-reasoning_SFT|est]] on the seven tasks at 100 words, with [[gain7_first100_Phi-4-reasoning_SFT|n]] of 350 pairs kept).
%%%% DEGEN
 On the seven tasks, scoring degenerate traces 0 gives Qwen gains of [[gain7_degen0_SFT_seed_42|est]], [[gain7_degen0_SFT_seed_1|est]] and [[gain7_degen0_SFT_seed_2|est]] (Table~\ref{tab:models}).
%%%% HEAD1
The CoT-Control grader treats a heading line as a sentence, so a heading can pass or fail a constraint by itself. Grading every trace with a leading heading removed raises the seven-task SFT gains over Base from [[gain7_SFT_seed_42|est]], [[gain7_SFT_seed_1|est]] and [[gain7_SFT_seed_2|est]] to [[gain7_stripped_SFT_seed_42|est]], [[gain7_stripped_SFT_seed_1|est]] and [[gain7_stripped_SFT_seed_2|est]] (ten tasks: $+11.77$, $+13.16$ and $+13.64$), mostly on meow (2.0\% to 22.0\% for seed 42) and end of sentence (0\% to 10.0\%), so the reported gains are conservative; on ReasonIF, removing the heading changes IFS by at most one point. Table~\ref{tab:headings} grades every run this way.

We retrained the reported recipe three more times (one run each). With all 537 headings deleted from the GPT-5.2 data, the seven-task gain disappears ([[gain7_stripped_SFT_no_headers|ci]]); with every heading replaced by one fixed \texttt{**Reasoning**} heading, compliance falls below Base ([[gain7_stripped_SFT_neutral_header|ci]]). Both models copy the summaries' first-person opening sentence (``I need to\ldots''), which lowers third person and lowercase below Base. Conversely, placing each prompt's GPT-5.2 opening heading in front of the Qwen3-235B-A22B reasoning (the heading text is copied from the GPT-5.2 example with the same prompt, and the Qwen3-235B-A22B reasoning and answer are left unchanged; nothing is regenerated), which alone gives no gain ([[gain7_stripped_SFT_teacher_qwen3_235b|ci]]) and no significant ReasonIF gain, restores part of the effect: [[gain7_stripped_SFT_header_transplant|ci]] on the seven tasks against [[gain7_stripped_SFT_seed_42|est]] for the GPT-5.2 summaries, and the full ReasonIF gain (34.7\% IFS, $+20.7$, $[15.0, 26.3]$), with outputs longer than the GPT-5.2 adapter's (mean 3,531 against 2,024 tokens). Over all ten tasks the transplant matches the GPT-5.2 adapter ($+12.57$, $[10.40, 14.85]$), mostly through the case tasks. For Qwen3-235B-A22B, the other-teacher contrast of Table~\ref{tab:variants} is therefore partly one of format rather than teacher. Headings are not the only route, however: Claude Sonnet 4.5 reasoning, which has none, raises seven-task compliance by [[gain7_SFT_teacher_sonnet_3_7|ci]] ([[gain7_degen0_SFT_teacher_sonnet_3_7|est]] with degenerate traces scored 0) while leaving ReasonIF unchanged, and the adapter trained with the loss on the reasoning only writes a heading in every trace but gains little ([[gain7_SFT_output_mask|ci]]; Appendix Table~\ref{tab:seven-all}). On ReasonIF the fixed heading keeps the gain (38.0\%), so there the heading's content matters less than on CoT-Control.

On the case tasks, the headings help because the model fills them in.
%%%% HEAD2
 This account concerns the three case tasks, where the opening's case can be checked directly; they are the tasks that do not predict obfuscation in \citet{haskins2026obfuscation}. On the seven other tasks the heading experiments show that the headings matter, but not how.
%%%% TEACHER
\subsection{Does the Teacher Matter?}
\label{sec:teacher}
The other-teacher runs of Table~\ref{tab:variants} keep the GPT-5.2 prompts but change the reasoning in several ways at once. To test whether a teacher's own controllability carries over, we trained the reported recipe on the same 212 questions with reasoning from two teachers at the extremes of \citet{chen2026cotcontrol}: Claude 3.7 Sonnet (51.1\% stylistic and 49.9\% addition compliance in their Table 2) and DeepSeek R1 (0.2\% and 0.0\%). The reasoning comes from the public s1K releases (\texttt{simplescaling/s1K-claude-3-7-sonnet} and \texttt{simplescaling/s1K-1.1}), which pair the same s1K questions (mostly mathematics and science) with each teacher's raw reasoning, not a summary. We kept the [[s1k_eligible]] questions whose traces from both teachers fit a 12,288-token training cutoff (the s1K traces are much longer than the summaries) and that share no 8-gram with CoT-Control, and drew 212 at random (seed 42); the cutoff and a batch of $1\times8$ are the only changes to the recipe. ReasonIF is not evaluated, because its questions overlap with the sources of s1K.

The Claude student gains [[gain7_SFT_s1k_claude37|ci]] on the seven tasks, also on the first 100 words ([[gain7_first100_SFT_s1k_claude37|est]]) and with degenerate traces scored 0 ([[gain7_degen0_SFT_s1k_claude37|est]]), whereas the R1 student does not change ([[gain7_SFT_s1k_deepseek_r1|ci]]); paired, the Claude student is [[claude37_minus_r1_7|ci]] points above the R1 student (Table~\ref{tab:teacher}). Its gain is on word suppression ([[task_mean_Base_word_suppression]]\% to [[task_mean_SFT_s1k_claude37_word_suppression]]\%) and the arrow prefix ([[task_mean_Base_arrow_prefix]]\% to [[task_mean_SFT_s1k_claude37_arrow_prefix]]\%), which the GPT-5.2 summaries do not move, and its traces are longer than Base's ([[capped_SFT_s1k_claude37]] of 500 reach the token limit, against [[capped_Base]]). Fine-tuning on Qwen3-14B's own reasoning for the GPT-5.2 prompts gives no gain ([[gain7_SFT_self_distill|ci]]). With the same questions, then, which teacher wrote the reasoning changes the student's controllability, and summaries are not required. For Claude Sonnet 4.5 the comparison is less clear: it is near zero on stylistic and addition constraints in \citet{chen2026cotcontrol} (0.2\% and 0.0\%) but not on suppression (15.6\%, close to Claude 3.7 Sonnet's 15.0\%), and its student's seven-task gain ([[gain7_SFT_teacher_sonnet_3_7|ci]]) lies mostly on the two suppression tasks and on repeat sentences, many of whose passes are degenerate ([[gain7_degen0_SFT_teacher_sonnet_3_7|est]] with degenerate traces scored 0); its prompts, length and format also differ from the s1K runs. Both s1K students and the self-distilled model lose most of the base model's lowercase compliance ([[lower_Base]]\% to [[lower_SFT_s1k_claude37]]\%, [[lower_SFT_s1k_deepseek_r1]]\% and [[lower_SFT_self_distill]]\%). All of these are single runs on one student model.
%%%% CROSS1
On the seven tasks, with base-derived openings Qwen's continuation compliance is [[cross7_Qwen3-14B_BB_mean]]\% for the base recipient and [[cross7_Qwen3-14B_BS_mean]]\% for the SFT recipient; with SFT-derived openings it is [[cross7_Qwen3-14B_SB_mean]]\% and [[cross7_Qwen3-14B_SS_mean]]\% (ten tasks: 16.11\%, 15.76\%, 29.51\% and 25.26\%; Figure~\ref{fig:haskins-crossed}). Changing the donor from Base to SFT raises the unchanged recipient's compliance by [[cross7_Qwen3-14B_SB_minus_BB|ci]] points (ten tasks: $+13.40$), while changing the recipient from Base to SFT does not help with the base donor ([[cross7_Qwen3-14B_BS_minus_BB|ci]]) or the SFT donor ([[cross7_Qwen3-14B_SS_minus_SB|ci]]; ten tasks: $-0.35$ and $-4.25$). Fifteen SFT$\to$Base and 14 SFT$\to$SFT openings already close the reasoning; excluding them and every unfinished continuation (420 pairs), the ten-task recipient effect is unchanged ($-4.62$, $[-7.43, -1.68]$).
%%%% CROSS2
Prefix-OFF, in which the donor is not given the constraint and its opening names no rule, still reaches 32.0\% IFS ($+18.0$ over Base, $[12.7, 23.3]$; $-1.3$ against SFT, $[-6.3, 3.7]$), and on the seven CoT-Control tasks it keeps most of the donor effect: [[cross7_Qwen3-14B_A5_mean]]\% against [[cross7_Qwen3-14B_BB_mean]]\% after the base donor ([[cross7_Qwen3-14B_A5_minus_BB|ci]]) and [[cross7_Qwen3-14B_A1rerun_mean]]\% for Prefix-ON in the same pipeline ([[cross7_Qwen3-14B_A5_minus_A1rerun|ci]]). Only over all ten tasks does Prefix-OFF fall well short of Prefix-ON (19.95\% against 29.71\%), through the case tasks. On both benchmarks, then, most of what the SFT opening transfers on the tasks that predict obfuscation does not depend on the donor having seen the constraint.

Phi's opening does not transfer: on the seven tasks the SFT donor changes the base recipient's compliance by [[cross7_Phi-4-reasoning_SB_minus_BB|ci]] points ([[cross7_Phi-4-reasoning_BB_mean]]\% to [[cross7_Phi-4-reasoning_SB_mean]]\%), whereas the SFT recipient does better than the base recipient with either donor ([[cross7_Phi-4-reasoning_BS_minus_BB|ci]]; [[cross7_Phi-4-reasoning_SS_minus_SB|ci]]),
%%%% CTRL1
Over these eight tasks, the transferred ingredient is therefore mainly constraint-specific text at the start of the reasoning, which the SFT donor writes more often.
%%%% CTRL2
These eight tasks include the three case tasks. On the five style tasks that are not character-level (Table~\ref{tab:controls}), the SFT donor raises compliance over the base donor by [[controls5_SB_vs_BB|ci]] points, but an opening that only names the rule gives nothing ([[controls5_named_vs_BB|ci]] against the base donor), and neither does one that obeys a different rule ([[controls5_mismatched_vs_BB|ci]]). The SFT-donor opening written for another question matches the SFT donor ([[controls5_cross_question_vs_SB|ci]]), as does the most frequent SFT opening of the task ([[controls5_modal_sft_vs_SB|ci]]), and only an opening that itself obeys the rule goes further ([[controls5_handcrafted_vs_SB|ci]] over the SFT donor). On these tasks what the SFT opening transfers is its general style rather than constraint-specific content, consistent with the Prefix-OFF result above.
%%%% DISC
The ablations narrow down why, more clearly on the case tasks than on the seven tasks that predict obfuscation. On the case tasks, compliance is largely decided at the start of the reasoning, in the base model as well as after fine-tuning: training reasoning that begins with a short, free-form span the student can write in the required style (the GPT-5.2 headings, Claude Sonnet 4.5's short opening sentences) raises it, and fixed or ordinary capitalized openings lower it. On the seven other tasks, deleting the headings removes the gain but adding them to another teacher's reasoning restores only part of it, an SFT opening helps whether or not its writer saw the constraint, and the teacher matters: with the same questions, Claude 3.7 Sonnet reasoning raises compliance and DeepSeek R1 reasoning does not. Which property of the training reasoning carries the effect on these tasks remains open.
%%%% LIMIT
 We report the seven-task composite as the main CoT-Control number following \citet{haskins2026obfuscation}, a choice made after seeing the ten-task results; both are in every table. The teacher test uses one pair of teachers on one student, with a longer training cutoff and different questions than the main runs, and the two Claude teachers also differ in prompts, length and format, so it cannot isolate the teacher's controllability.
